.","page_number":"Reproduced: p. 7","url":"https://archive.org/details/CMAHandbook1991/page/n22"}],"collapse_artists":false,"collection":"China - Zhou Dynasty","conservation_statement":null,"copyright":null,"cover_accession_number":null,"creation_date":"early 400s BCE","creation_date_earliest":-499,"creation_date_latest":-475,"creators":[],"creditline":"John L. Severance Fund","culture":["China, Eastern Zhou dynasty (770–256 BCE)"],"current_location":null,"date_added_to_oa":null,"date_text":"early 400s BCE","department":"Chinese Art","description":"Music in the Eastern Zhou dynasty not only served ritual purposes but also provided entertainment in feudal courts. The elliptical cross-section of the Chinese bronze bell is unique; it could produce two notes depending on where the bell is struck, either at the rim or near the center. The bell is suspended when played and often belongs to a graduated set arranged according to pitch and size. This bell displays elaborate ornamentation and exquisite workmanship. A twin-bodied serpent intertwined with two tigers forms the suspension loop at the top. Coiled serpents form the knobs on the body. The lower part bears an animal mask, its horns interlaced with abstract bird designs.","did_you_know":"The bell could produce two notes depending on where it is struck.","dimensions":{"overall":{"height":0.414,"height_inch":16,"height_inch_fraction":0.3125}},"early_education_description":null,"edition_of_the_work":null,"exhibitions":{"current":[{"description":"<i>Year in Review - 1962</i>. The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH (organizer) (October 24-November 25, 1962).","id":302276,"opening_date":"1962-10-24T04:00:00","title":"Year in Review - 1962"},{"description":"<i>Asian Autumn: New Objects/New Insights: Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions</i>. The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH (organizer) (October 18-December 31, 1994).","id":311620,"opening_date":"1994-10-18T04:00:00","title":"Asian Autumn: New Objects/New Insi